how do you rebuild after covid when you  can't get the workers to do the job  it's a central question for the economy  and one they're grappling with on the  ground on this site in wooleton in  nottingham  they're building 16 homes here but  securing the right trades people at the  right time is a challenge one that's  getting more expensive  the sector has struggled for a long  period of time now with uh with the  skill shortage it's nothing new  um it's been there for a number of years  but you know on the back of everything  that's happened with covid and with  brexit we seem to have  created a real sort of situation that  isn't improving  with disrupted supply chains struggling  to meet resurgent demand the labor  market is in uncharted territory  the combination of low unemployment and  record vacancies more than 1.3 million  unfilled jobs in the economy ought to be  good news for workers forcing employers  to pay them more to attract talent and  wages are rising for many problem is  they're not rising fast enough to keep  up with inflation and the cost of living  the latest figures show real wages  judged against the headline rate of  consumer price inflation have fallen  faster and further than at any time in  two decades to minus 4.5 per cent  the trent bridge the cricket was  inflation proof tickets were free  ensuring a full house to watch england  win and a busy day for staff at the  larwood and vos pub hospitality has  offered some of the steepest pay rises  of any sector but it can still be a  struggle  we've not just followed the rises we  perhaps stayed ahead of them in some  cases we're paying people more  because i think it's the right thing to  do but  that then has to be followed with price  rises for everything that we sell  so you know the price of a pint is  probably more than it's ever been food  prices are going up so  you know it's a tough little equilibrium  the workforce is also missing almost  half a million people who can't or don't  want to work some of them because  postcovid priorities have  changed without them there may be no  easy economic wins paul kelsey's sky  news in nottingham
 

